 # Optimization Procedures
+
 Also called: Compute Matching at Intervals, Interval-based Optimal Transport Update, Optimal Transport Matching Interval (K), matching interval
 
 ## Overview
-This family defines the iterative optimization procedures that minimize a photometric loss between rendered images and ground-truth observations by updating scene parameters (geometry, materials, illumination, or radiance field weights) [S1]. It solves the inverse problem of recovering 3D scene properties from 2D data by treating the rendering process as a differentiable function [S1, S2, S3]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by providing the generic loop structure (forward render, loss compute, backward pass, update) upon which specific strategies like [NeRF Training Loop](970-nerf-training-loop.md), [Optimization Configuration (Adam + Exponential Decay + Selective L2)](2133-optimization-configuration-adam-exponential-decay-selective-.md), and [Photometric Optimization](5790-photometric-optimization.md) are built.
+Optimization Procedures define the iterative algorithmic framework and specific strategies used to solve inverse rendering problems by minimizing the discrepancy between rendered images and ground-truth observations [S1]. This family solves the problem of estimating scene parameters (geometry, appearance, lighting, camera poses) by treating the differentiable renderer as a loss function evaluator [S1]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by providing the generic control flow—sampling, rendering, loss computation, and parameter update—upon which specific strategies like NeRF training, black-box optimization, or staged refinement are built, rather than defining the specific loss functions or parameter representations themselves.
 
