 ### [Active Set Method for OIT-based 3DGS](7528-active-set-method-for-oit-based-3dgs.md)
-The Active Set Method for OIT-based 3DGS (SparseOIT) is an optimization strategy that accelerates the training of Order-Independent Transparency (OIT) based 3D Gaussian Splatting by exploiting the sparsity of variable dependencies among Gaussian splats. Unlike standard 3DGS which sorts all Gaussians per pixel, SparseOIT removes the depth sorting step entirely and freezes Gaussians that have converged or contribute negligibly to the gradient, optimizing only a subset of "active" variables. This approach achieves comparable novel-view quality to volumetric rendering methods while significantly outperforming existing OIT-based methods in training speed.
+The Active Set Method for OIT-based 3DGS (SparseOIT) is an optimization strategy that accelerates the training of Order-Independent Transparency (OIT) based 3D Gaussian Splatting by exploiting the sparsity of variable dependencies among Gaussian splats. Unlike standard 3DGS which requires per-pixel depth sorting, SparseOIT removes the sorting step entirely and freezes Gaussians that have converged or contribute negligibly to the gradient, optimizing only a subset of "active" variables. This approach achieves comparable novel-view quality to volumetric rendering methods while significantly outperforming existing OIT-based methods in training speed.
 
 ### [Photometric Reconstruction Loss](7799-photometric-reconstruction-loss.md)
-Photometric reconstruction loss minimizes the difference between rendered pixels and input training images during joint optimization of scene representation and camera poses. It serves as the primary supervisory signal for differentiable volume rendering, ensuring that the reconstructed radiance field accurately reproduces the observed views. Unlike sibling methods that focus on initialization or regularization, this loss directly drives the geometric and photometric consistency of the scene by penalizing pixel-wise errors.
+Photometric Reconstruction Loss minimizes the error between rendered pixel colors and input training images during joint optimization of scene representation and camera parameters. It distinguishes itself from standard Mean Squared Error (MSE) losses by explicitly mapping 3D sample coordinates from a canonical ray to camera-specific rays via rigid transformations, ensuring geometric consistency across views. Unlike sibling methods that may rely solely on pixel-wise intensity matching without explicit 3D warping, this method enforces that the radiance field evaluated at the transformed 3D position matches the observed image color.
+
+### [Bundle Adjustment](8540-bundle-adjustment.md)
+Bundle Adjustment (BA) in the context of differentiable volume rendering and neural radiance fields is an optimization procedure that iteratively refines camera pose parameters (quaternion and translation) for a given view by minimizing the pixel-wise loss between the rendered image and the ground truth image. It solves the problem of inaccurate or missing camera poses, which are typically required inputs for volume rendering but may be noisy or entirely absent in uncalibrated scenarios. Unlike sibling methods that may rely on radiometric calibration or joint optimization of neural fields with complex geometric constraints [Siblings], this method specifically targets the geometric alignment of the camera extrinsics using gradient descent on the differentiable rendering output. It sets itself apart by treating the camera pose as a learnable tensor optimized directly against the photometric error of the volume render, often employing specific learning rate schedules and masking strategies to handle sparse or zero-valued pixels.
+
+### [Adversarial and GAN-based Optimization](12094-adversarial-and-gan-based-optimization.md)
+Adversarial and GAN-based Optimization methods enhance the photorealism and structural consistency of radiance fields by integrating a discriminator network into the training loop. Unlike standard photometric optimization which minimizes pixel-wise reconstruction errors, these methods enforce that rendered image patches follow the distribution of real image patches, thereby suppressing artifacts and improving high-frequency details. This family is distinct from pure geometric or photometric approaches [44-radiance-field-optimization.md] by introducing a game-theoretic component that provides gradient feedback for perceptual quality rather than just color accuracy.
+
+### Compute Matching at Intervals
+An optimization strategy to improve speed by computing the pixel matching (transportation matrix T) less frequently than the gradient updates [S1]. Computing pixel matching is costly and is the bottleneck in computation, while there are only subtle changes in pixel matching within adjacent iteration steps [S1]. Instead of computing the matching at each step, it is computed at intervals (i.e., once every $K+1$ steps) for higher speed without hurting accuracy [S1]. The matching interval $K$ controls the frequency of recomputing pixel matching during optimization to accelerate convergence [S1]. During optimization, optimal transport matching computation is skipped for $K-1$ iterations, then recomputed on the $K$-th iteration [S1]. The parameter $K$ is the matching interval; $K=0$ computes matching at every iteration, while $K=5$ is the default, providing a trade-off between speed and quality [S1]. Setting $K=5$ or $K=10$ generally leads to similar result qualities compared to $K=0$, while increasing the running speed by $4\times$ to $5\times$ [S1].
+
+### Hybrid Gradients
+A hybrid optimizing strategy that combines RGBXY derivatives and standard derivatives, leveraging the advantages of both [S1]. RGBXY derivatives are highly effective in capturing global and long-range influence, but may be less effective when initial and target shapes are already aligned very well, potentially due to inaccurate matching from the approximated optimal transport method when shapes are close [S1]. The hybrid strategy uses RGBXY derivatives for the first 75% of iterations and switches to standard derivatives for the last 25% of iterations [S1]. A randomized strategy for combining them (randomly selecting one at each iteration) does not show benefits [S1].
+
+### Per-Scene Fine-tuning
+A post-processing step where the predicted voxel features of the global radiance field are optimized per scene to boost rendering quality [S2]. The process takes the global radiance field $V_g$ predicted by the network and optimizes the predicted voxel features per scene using rendering losses for a short period [S2]. Fine-tuning takes approximately 20 minutes to 1 hour, resulting in state-of-the-art quality that outperforms NeRF [S2].
+
+### Two-Stage Optimization with Edge-Aware Surface Rendering
+A staged optimization procedure for inverse rendering. Stage 1 trains a volumetric radiance field for 100k iterations, sampling 512 random pixels per iteration, and optimizes using L1 image loss and eikonal regularization loss with weight $\lambda_1 = 0.1$ [S3]. Stage 2 trains an edge-aware physics-based surface rendering for 50k iterations, rendering a random $128 \times 128$ image patch per iteration, and optimizes using L2 image loss over 4 Gaussian pyramid levels, eikonal loss ($\lambda_1 = 0.1$), and roughness range loss ($\lambda_2 = 0.1$) [S3]. The depth gradient magnitude threshold for edge localization is $\tau = 10^{-2}$, the maximum number of surface walk steps is $K = 16$, the step size for surface walk is $\epsilon = 10^{-3}$, and the dot-product threshold for edge point localization is $\delta = 5 \times 10^{-2}$ [S3]. The BRDF distribution parameter is 'ggx', with intIOR, extIOR, and nonlinear parameters using Mitsuba roughplastic defaults [S3]. Total training time for both stages is ~10 hours on a single NVIDIA RTX 2080 Ti GPU with 12GB memory [S3].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of optimizing radiance fields via differentiable rendering was pioneered by Mildenhall et al. in the original NeRF paper (2020) [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. Subsequent work introduced various optimization strategies to improve convergence speed, stability, and memory efficiency. The specific technique of computing optimal transport matching at intervals was introduced by Liu et al. in "Differentiable Rendering Using RGBXY Derivatives and Optimal Transport" (2022) [S1]. This method addresses the computational bottleneck of frequent pixel matching computations during optimization.
+The optimization procedures described herein originate from various specific research papers addressing different aspects of radiance field reconstruction. The interval-based optimal transport update and hybrid gradients are detailed in "Differentiable Rendering Using RGBXY Derivatives and Optimal Transport" [S1]. The per-scene fine-tuning approach is introduced in "NeRFusion: Fusing Radiance Fields for Large-Scale Scene Reconstruction" [S2]. The two-stage optimization with edge-aware surface rendering is presented in "IRON: Inverse Rendering by Optimizing Neural SDFs and Materials from Photometric Images" [S3]. Other methods in this family, such as Bundle Adjustment and Active Set Methods for 3DGS, are developed within the broader context of neural rendering and Gaussian Splatting literature [S1].
 
